WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.843 --> 00:00:06.260
BALAJI RAJAM: Thank
you, everyone,

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:09.140
for joining the session on
"Accelerating application

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:11.300
development with Anthos."

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:13.590
This is a rough
agenda for today.

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:15.780
We'll have a quick introduction.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:18.530
We'll talk about challenges
of application delivery today.

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:21.530
We'll talk about application
development with Anthos.

00:00:21.530 --> 00:00:25.730
And then, we will get into
how enterprises can migrate

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:28.786
from PCF using kf and Anthos.

00:00:28.786 --> 00:00:30.760
A quick introduction
about myself.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:32.240
My name is Balaji Rajam.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:35.077
I've been at Google
Cloud since 2016.

00:00:35.077 --> 00:00:37.535
I am a Solutions Manager with
the Application Modernization

00:00:37.535 --> 00:00:39.830
Team.

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:42.710
First, let's talk about the
challenges of application

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:44.150
delivery today.

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:46.910
We have been talking to
customers about the challenges,

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:48.140
what they are facing.

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:52.280
And they have talked to us
about their business objectives,

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:55.560
as well as their
technology objectives.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:57.920
As far as their
business objectives go,

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:00.260
most enterprises are
looking to accelerate time

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:03.830
to market for new
capabilities and features.

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:05.450
And especially in
the current climate,

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:07.410
costs are on top of their minds.

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:10.520
So they are looking to
reduce costs as well.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:13.350
How this translates into
technology objectives is they

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:17.690
are trying to drive speed
and agility using automation.

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:21.020
They are trying to improve
developer efficiency

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:23.240
through self-service.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:27.320
They want to have a unified
operating models across on-prem

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:31.370
and hybrid or multi-clouds
because most organizations have

00:01:31.370 --> 00:01:33.440
started adopting public clouds.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:37.250
And security is also top
of mind for most folks.

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:40.130
So they want to ensure
whatever solution they choose

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:44.590
has the best-in-class
security and compliance.

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:46.960
But if you look at those
points, traditional software

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:49.720
development and
testing and deployment

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:52.810
has not really kept
up with those demands.

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:56.930
Let's see, look at some of
the reasons why it has not.

00:01:56.930 --> 00:01:59.260
Traditional enterprise
software development

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:02.770
was developed for
the on-prem work.

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:04.570
So it has several
limitations when

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:08.199
it comes to addressing most
modern application delivery

00:02:08.199 --> 00:02:10.150
models.

00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:12.310
Traditional enterprise
software development

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:16.570
relies on imperative thinking,
where things are much more

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:19.780
specifically defined, whereas,
more modern application

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:23.110
development relies on
a declarative approach.

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:26.380
Previously in
enterprises, applications,

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:29.260
once they're developed, they go
through a security evaluation

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:30.580
process.

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:33.310
And that is how they get
approved for deployment.

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:35.890
And that usually comes towards
the end of the software

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:37.420
development lifecycle.

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:39.220
What that means is
it becomes a choice

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:41.050
between fixing the
security issues

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:43.810
and going into production.

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:46.060
Enterprises are looking to
change that and incorporate

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:48.880
security into every phase of
their software development

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:50.730
lifecycle.

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:52.680
Increasingly, companies
are also starting

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:54.820
to adopt open source software.

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:59.190
And that is causing them
to rethink how they test

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:02.770
as well as deploy software.

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:04.600
The typical governance
models, which

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:07.740
are in use within
organizations, were created

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:09.520
for the on-premise work.

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:11.410
And those have
not really kept up

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:14.140
with the kind of rapid
deployments, which

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:17.680
you see in companies who
have adopted public cloud.

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:22.010
And those also need to change.

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:25.250
DevOps Research and Assessment
is an organization, which

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:27.470
does research on these topics.

00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:31.220
And this is a company, which
Google acquired a little while

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:32.550
ago.

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:35.870
They published a report
in 2019, after talking

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:38.960
to a lot of enterprises
about the state of DevOps

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:40.940
in those respective
organizations.

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:43.560
And they had some key findings.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:46.950
The first one was delivering
software quickly, reliably,

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:50.510
and safely is at the heart
of technology transformation.

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:54.780
And it really effects the
organization performance.

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:56.810
Most companies come
from the on-prem world.

00:03:56.810 --> 00:04:00.890
And they bring that approach
to public cloud as well.

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:04.830
And that is not conducive
to optimum performance.

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:09.090
So how you implement your cloud
infrastructure really matters.

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:11.420
The other one is the change
approval process, which

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:13.760
are prevalent in enterprises.

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:16.550
And those can be
pretty antiquated,

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:20.660
which goes through many
hoops before you can get

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:22.700
approved on the cloud software.

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:26.270
And that has to be
reduced, so that you

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:29.060
can cater to the modern
development approaches, which

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:31.338
are prevalent today.

00:04:31.338 --> 00:04:35.170
So when the DORA team
looked at the organizations

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:37.180
and tried to evaluate
them, they evaluated them

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:40.180
on four key parameters.

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:44.350
The first one is the lead
time required for deployment.

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:48.700
The second one is how
often can you deploy.

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:52.690
The third one is how quickly
you can recover and change

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:54.160
in case of a failure.

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:58.660
And the last one was what is
the time required to restore

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:02.490
in clear case of an issue.

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:05.312
So they broke these metrics
into different categories.

00:05:05.312 --> 00:05:06.770
And based on these
parameters, they

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:11.040
classified organizations into
elite, high, medium, and low.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:13.280
And if you look at the
elite organizations,

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:15.920
they have on-demand
deployments, where

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:18.200
they can do multiple
deployments per day.

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:22.040
And they have a change
failure rate of less than 15%.

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:24.530
And when you come to the
low-performing organizations,

00:05:24.530 --> 00:05:27.480
they deploy maybe once a month
or, in the worst case scenario,

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:29.030
once every six months.

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:32.400
And they have change failure
rates which are really bad.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:34.780
But if you look at the
majority of the organizations,

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:35.780
they fall in the middle.

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:39.140
Around 67% of organizations
they evaluated fall

00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:40.970
in the high or medium category.

00:05:40.970 --> 00:05:42.950
But all of these
organizations have a desire

00:05:42.950 --> 00:05:47.820
to shift left and become more
elite in their performance.

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:50.300
So this is where
developing with Anthos

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:53.510
comes in because Anthos was
built from the ground up,

00:05:53.510 --> 00:05:57.370
to cater to these
kind of requirements.

00:05:57.370 --> 00:06:01.560
So Anthos was the result of 15
years of Google's innovation

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:05.940
in this area, right from 2003,
when Google engineers created

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:09.780
Borg, which, again, became
open sourced as Kubernetes, all

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:14.510
the way up to 2019, when
Anthos was released.

00:06:14.510 --> 00:06:19.460
So let's see how Anthos enables
cloud-native development today.

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:22.130
The first aspect which a lot
of our customers are looking at

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:23.820
is security.

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:25.340
They want separation
of duties, so

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:28.180
that developers and the
activities they'll do

00:06:28.180 --> 00:06:30.770
are separated from the
activities, which the operators

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:32.570
and deployment folks do.

00:06:32.570 --> 00:06:34.970
They want effective
vulnerability management

00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:39.050
and being able to have
effective access controls.

00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:42.800
They want to be able to
define their infrastructure,

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:48.120
both as code as well as
the application platform.

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:52.190
It allows them to automate
the activities which they do,

00:06:52.190 --> 00:06:54.560
so that it is better
auditable and it can

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:57.530
be controlled better as well.

00:06:57.530 --> 00:06:59.420
Effective deployment
of applications

00:06:59.420 --> 00:07:02.300
mean that they
are containerized,

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:05.250
so that they can be isolated
from other applications

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:07.460
and they are extensible
and transparent.

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:09.200
And most organizations
are concerned

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:12.690
about being locked into
a particular vendor.

00:07:12.690 --> 00:07:16.290
So it has to be
open-standards based.

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:18.840
And Anthos satisfies
all these criteria.

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:21.380
So let's look at
how Anthos enables

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:24.410
elite DevOps performance today.

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:26.630
It reduces the
developer platform

00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:29.105
because it shields
developers from knowing

00:07:29.105 --> 00:07:30.980
the details of the
platform, so that they can

00:07:30.980 --> 00:07:33.090
focus on value-add activities.

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:34.980
It establish governance
and separation

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:38.060
of roles across
application life cycle.

00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:42.890
And organizations are able to
centrally control configuration

00:07:42.890 --> 00:07:46.010
and policies across
applications and themes.

00:07:46.010 --> 00:07:49.430
It also allows them to
leverage existing investments

00:07:49.430 --> 00:07:52.850
and skill sets because
Anthos is based Kubernetes.

00:07:52.850 --> 00:07:56.000
And it can run on
any cloud platform.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:58.490
And it also provides
them the flexibility

00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:01.430
to build, test, and
run their applications

00:08:01.430 --> 00:08:02.517
wherever they choose.

00:08:02.517 --> 00:08:04.100
So these are a couple
of our customers

00:08:04.100 --> 00:08:08.180
who have used Anthos to increase
their DevOps performance.

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:11.270
Kaeser Kompressoren is one
of the leading manufacturers

00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:12.710
of compressors in the world.

00:08:12.710 --> 00:08:15.770
They use Anthos to modernize
existing applications

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:18.670
on their current infrastructure.

00:08:18.670 --> 00:08:20.550
HSBC used it
slightly differently.

00:08:20.550 --> 00:08:24.110
They used Anthos to accelerate
their software velocity

00:08:24.110 --> 00:08:25.826
in a hybrid environment.

00:08:25.826 --> 00:08:31.090
Next, let's look at how you
can modernize and migrate

00:08:31.090 --> 00:08:36.190
your Cloud Foundry applications
using Anthos and kf.

00:08:36.190 --> 00:08:39.590
This applies to both Pivotal
Cloud Foundry applications,

00:08:39.590 --> 00:08:43.669
as well as the open-source
Cloud Foundry applications.

00:08:43.669 --> 00:08:46.650
So when we spoke
to PCF customers,

00:08:46.650 --> 00:08:49.000
this is some of the
insights we had.

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:51.730
PCF came into popularity
a few years ago.

00:08:51.730 --> 00:08:54.400
And it gained a large
mindshare with customers,

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:58.060
predominantly because
developers really

00:08:58.060 --> 00:09:00.285
loved the opinionated workflow.

00:09:00.285 --> 00:09:03.940
Developers loved the fact that
they can compile their app,

00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:04.740
build it.

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:08.860
They can turn it into
an image and deploy it,

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:13.220
in a short while, through a
simple command, like cf push.

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:15.620
But at the same
time, operators have

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:19.590
been struggling with managing
Cloud Foundry workloads.

00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:23.420
So for instance, BOSH, which
forms the underlying technology

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:26.210
of Cloud Foundry,
is not as flexible

00:09:26.210 --> 00:09:30.050
or as general purpose as a
platform like Kubernetes.

00:09:30.050 --> 00:09:32.450
And [? populators ?]
are increasingly

00:09:32.450 --> 00:09:35.600
getting used to using more
declarative approaches

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:37.820
to application deployment.

00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:41.300
And they want that same out
of all of their platforms.

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:44.570
And most organizations have
started to adopt Kubernetes.

00:09:44.570 --> 00:09:46.730
So now, Cloud
Foundry becomes yet

00:09:46.730 --> 00:09:48.190
another platform to maintain.

00:09:48.190 --> 00:09:50.690
So they are maintaining a Cloud
Foundry platform, as well as

00:09:50.690 --> 00:09:52.310
a Kubernetes platform.

00:09:52.310 --> 00:09:56.120
And especially if you're
using Pivotal's Cloud Foundry,

00:09:56.120 --> 00:09:58.550
it means that you are
paying a lot in licensing

00:09:58.550 --> 00:10:00.580
cost or subscription cost.

00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:04.410
The model of Pivotal is based
on application instances.

00:10:04.410 --> 00:10:07.860
So every time you deploy a
new application instance,

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:11.060
you have to pay a
subscription cost to Pivotal.

00:10:11.060 --> 00:10:14.840
So that essentially
discourages architectures,

00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:18.920
like using micro services,
because that is predicated

00:10:18.920 --> 00:10:22.670
on deploying multiple instances
of an application to scale-up

00:10:22.670 --> 00:10:24.540
and so on.

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:27.380
So given that, people are
looking for better ways

00:10:27.380 --> 00:10:30.540
to do that application
development.

00:10:30.540 --> 00:10:33.315
So we spoke to people
from different parts

00:10:33.315 --> 00:10:34.190
of the organizations.

00:10:34.190 --> 00:10:39.140
The two personas we focused on
was developers and operators.

00:10:39.140 --> 00:10:41.150
And we focused on what
are the things which

00:10:41.150 --> 00:10:42.620
are important to them.

00:10:42.620 --> 00:10:44.210
So for the developer,
they really

00:10:44.210 --> 00:10:46.070
want an opinionated
workflow, where

00:10:46.070 --> 00:10:48.260
they don't have to worry
about the underpinnings

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:49.400
of the platform.

00:10:49.400 --> 00:10:52.880
They want to compile, build,
and deploy images easily.

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:54.710
They also want to be
platform agnostic,

00:10:54.710 --> 00:10:58.340
so that when the company decides
to move from one platform

00:10:58.340 --> 00:11:02.120
to the other, they do not
have to come back and change

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:03.470
something.

00:11:03.470 --> 00:11:07.523
They also want the option
to move to a zero-ops model.

00:11:07.523 --> 00:11:08.940
On the operator
side of the house,

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:10.730
they want really want
declarative platform

00:11:10.730 --> 00:11:12.140
management.

00:11:12.140 --> 00:11:15.260
Organizations which have
moved to a public cloud

00:11:15.260 --> 00:11:18.710
have used things like
Terraform to automate

00:11:18.710 --> 00:11:23.300
and make it more declarative
to create the infrastructure.

00:11:23.300 --> 00:11:25.710
So they want that same out of
their application platforms

00:11:25.710 --> 00:11:27.030
as well.

00:11:27.030 --> 00:11:29.000
They want to be able to
apply common policies

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:32.300
across enterprise, so that
they can standardize on how

00:11:32.300 --> 00:11:34.340
they operate the platforms.

00:11:34.340 --> 00:11:37.220
They want higher visibility
into microservices

00:11:37.220 --> 00:11:38.720
because enterprises
are increasingly

00:11:38.720 --> 00:11:40.400
adopting microservices.

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:43.310
And it is vital to them to
understand the information

00:11:43.310 --> 00:11:44.720
behind those.

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:47.780
And probably most importantly,
as most organizations

00:11:47.780 --> 00:11:50.450
are starting to
adopt public cloud,

00:11:50.450 --> 00:11:54.350
they are looking for ways to
limit their operational cost.

00:11:54.350 --> 00:11:56.210
And that can only
happen if you can

00:11:56.210 --> 00:11:59.340
have a unified control
plane across on-prem,

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:00.340
as well as public cloud.

00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:06.850
So this is where kf comes in.
kf was developed by Google,

00:12:06.850 --> 00:12:08.150
from the ground up.

00:12:08.150 --> 00:12:13.600
So what it allows developers to
do is use kf in the same manner

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:17.440
they would use CF commands or
the Cloud Foundry commands.

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:19.140
They can issue a
kf command, which

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:21.500
will interact with all
the underlying components

00:12:21.500 --> 00:12:25.610
of Anthos, to provide the same
experience which developers

00:12:25.610 --> 00:12:28.810
would have when they
use Cloud Foundry.

00:12:28.810 --> 00:12:30.340
Operators, on the
other hand, can

00:12:30.340 --> 00:12:31.900
use all the
declarative approaches

00:12:31.900 --> 00:12:34.720
provided by cubectl and
all the Kubernetes tools

00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:35.860
to manage the platform.

00:12:38.750 --> 00:12:40.430
So what is kf?

00:12:40.430 --> 00:12:44.620
So the kf is meant to provide
developers the Cloud Foundry

00:12:44.620 --> 00:12:47.710
experience, while empowering
operators to adopt

00:12:47.710 --> 00:12:49.360
declarative practices.

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:52.630
We allow organizations to
move their Cloud Foundry

00:12:52.630 --> 00:12:56.790
applications to Anthos,
without causing any disruption

00:12:56.790 --> 00:12:58.420
to their developers.

00:12:58.420 --> 00:13:01.330
kf is build by Google,
from the ground up,

00:13:01.330 --> 00:13:03.290
natively on Kubernetes.

00:13:03.290 --> 00:13:06.220
We do not use any of the
Cloud Foundry components.

00:13:06.220 --> 00:13:08.770
So that means that
organizations no longer

00:13:08.770 --> 00:13:12.220
have to pay the subscription
or licensing cost behind Cloud

00:13:12.220 --> 00:13:16.570
Foundry, say [INAUDIBLE].

00:13:16.570 --> 00:13:21.430
And administrators can operate
the platform using kubectl.

00:13:21.430 --> 00:13:24.820
And below are listed some of the
commands which are supported as

00:13:24.820 --> 00:13:26.930
part of CF.

00:13:26.930 --> 00:13:32.410
So when we built kf, we
actually brought the best of PCF

00:13:32.410 --> 00:13:33.760
to Anthos.

00:13:33.760 --> 00:13:37.820
We ensured we had provided a
CF-like developer workflow.

00:13:37.820 --> 00:13:40.690
We wanted to make sure that
we are building natively

00:13:40.690 --> 00:13:43.840
on top of Kubernetes, so that
we remove any other dependencies

00:13:43.840 --> 00:13:45.460
from Cloud Foundry.

00:13:45.460 --> 00:13:48.650
Buildpacks on a common
way how enterprises

00:13:48.650 --> 00:13:52.640
standardize, how their images
are built and deployed.

00:13:52.640 --> 00:13:54.880
So it is very important
to organizations

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:58.510
which use Cloud Foundry that we
provide support for buildpacks.

00:13:58.510 --> 00:14:01.180
And that is available
as part of kf.

00:14:01.180 --> 00:14:04.930
And we looked at the commonly
used commands from enterprises.

00:14:04.930 --> 00:14:09.790
And we added support
for 40-plus commands.

00:14:09.790 --> 00:14:12.737
And we also added support
for service brokers.

00:14:12.737 --> 00:14:14.320
And service brokers
are the mechanisms

00:14:14.320 --> 00:14:18.430
which CF applications
use to communicate

00:14:18.430 --> 00:14:19.820
with external open sources.

00:14:22.910 --> 00:14:26.030
Technical support for kf is
provided through all Google

00:14:26.030 --> 00:14:27.380
Cloud Support channels.

00:14:27.380 --> 00:14:30.510
There is no additional
cost to using kf.

00:14:30.510 --> 00:14:34.490
kf support is included as part
of your Anthos subscription.

00:14:34.490 --> 00:14:37.520
kf is supported both
on-prem, as well as on GCP

00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:39.560
and other public clouds.

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:43.860
And standard Google Cloud
deprecation policies apply.

00:14:43.860 --> 00:14:48.950
Let's look at a migration
from PCF to Anthos.

00:14:48.950 --> 00:14:51.150
So on the left-hand
side, in phase 1,

00:14:51.150 --> 00:14:55.250
you see the enterprise
status as of today, right,

00:14:55.250 --> 00:14:57.590
where they have CF
applications as well

00:14:57.590 --> 00:14:59.180
as Kubernetes application.

00:14:59.180 --> 00:15:01.040
And their platform
team is maintaining

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:02.280
two different platforms.

00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:03.500
One is Cloud Foundry.

00:15:03.500 --> 00:15:07.010
Another is probably a container
platform, like Kubernetes.

00:15:07.010 --> 00:15:09.470
So that means that the
operational overhead

00:15:09.470 --> 00:15:12.140
is high for the organization.

00:15:12.140 --> 00:15:15.620
In phase 2, when they start
adopting Anthos and kf,

00:15:15.620 --> 00:15:19.610
now they have a single platform
to maintain, which is Anthos.

00:15:19.610 --> 00:15:23.990
The operators running CF
application can use kf.

00:15:23.990 --> 00:15:26.660
And the folks running
Kubernetes applications

00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:30.770
can deploy their applications
on a Cloud Run or GKE,

00:15:30.770 --> 00:15:33.330
or Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:35.970
As the organization
develops a comfort level

00:15:35.970 --> 00:15:38.930
with Anthos and
GKE and Cloud Run,

00:15:38.930 --> 00:15:41.190
they can slowly start
migrating their Cloud Foundry

00:15:41.190 --> 00:15:44.910
applications from using
kf to running natively

00:15:44.910 --> 00:15:47.130
on Cloud Run or GKE.

00:15:47.130 --> 00:15:50.040
And we do not mandate
that organizations

00:15:50.040 --> 00:15:51.960
do that within a
certain amount of time.

00:15:51.960 --> 00:15:54.930
They can continue to
use kf until reach

00:15:54.930 --> 00:15:57.720
the level of comfort where they
can migrate their applications

00:15:57.720 --> 00:15:58.470
over.

00:15:58.470 --> 00:16:03.120
And in phase 4, you see the
enterprise has completely

00:16:03.120 --> 00:16:05.970
moved over, from Cloud
Foundry applications

00:16:05.970 --> 00:16:07.440
to Kubernetes application.

00:16:07.440 --> 00:16:11.790
And they are using
Cloud Run and GKE.

00:16:11.790 --> 00:16:14.420
Let's look now through
the various phases

00:16:14.420 --> 00:16:18.530
of PCF migration.

00:16:18.530 --> 00:16:20.180
So there are multiple
paths where you

00:16:20.180 --> 00:16:23.150
can migrate from PCF to Anthos.

00:16:23.150 --> 00:16:24.875
And we provide different
pathways for it,

00:16:24.875 --> 00:16:25.693
for enterprises.

00:16:25.693 --> 00:16:27.110
No enterprises are
built the same.

00:16:27.110 --> 00:16:28.890
And everybody has
their own challenges.

00:16:28.890 --> 00:16:30.130
So we acknowledge it.

00:16:30.130 --> 00:16:33.550
So certain enterprises feel
very comfortable moving straight

00:16:33.550 --> 00:16:39.180
away from PCF to something
like Cloud Run or GKE,

00:16:39.180 --> 00:16:43.455
either on Anthos
on-prem or on GCP,

00:16:43.455 --> 00:16:44.580
which is managed by Google.

00:16:44.580 --> 00:16:47.090
But other organizations
have their challenges

00:16:47.090 --> 00:16:49.940
because migrating hundreds of
apps built on Cloud Foundry

00:16:49.940 --> 00:16:51.810
is not that simple.

00:16:51.810 --> 00:16:54.270
So they can migrate
to Anthos and use

00:16:54.270 --> 00:16:57.260
kf, which will effectively
simplify the operation

00:16:57.260 --> 00:16:59.870
because they have only
one platform to manage,

00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:03.260
and continue on that pathway
of running the Cloud Foundry

00:17:03.260 --> 00:17:06.575
applications, until they feel
comfortable moving to Cloud Run

00:17:06.575 --> 00:17:07.160
or GKE.

00:17:11.260 --> 00:17:14.710
The first phase of moving
your Cloud Foundry application

00:17:14.710 --> 00:17:18.160
from Cloud Foundry to Anthos
is running an assessment.

00:17:18.160 --> 00:17:21.310
So as part of the kf tool,
we provide an assessment

00:17:21.310 --> 00:17:22.569
capability.

00:17:22.569 --> 00:17:26.150
You can run the kf tool against
your Cloud Foundry application.

00:17:26.150 --> 00:17:28.069
And it will actually
tell you what,

00:17:28.069 --> 00:17:31.670
if it is a good fit
for the kf platform.

00:17:31.670 --> 00:17:33.490
So in the next
slide, you actually

00:17:33.490 --> 00:17:37.960
see a sample report, which
is generated by the kf tool.

00:17:37.960 --> 00:17:40.150
And on the right-hand
side, it's actually

00:17:40.150 --> 00:17:44.470
showing whether the migration
assessment has passed or not.

00:17:44.470 --> 00:17:47.890
The next stage of using kf
to migrate your applications

00:17:47.890 --> 00:17:50.980
is now, to deploy
those applications.

00:17:50.980 --> 00:17:56.730
And you can do that through the
simple CLI of kf and kf push.

00:17:56.730 --> 00:17:59.700
And you can migrate your
existing applications

00:17:59.700 --> 00:18:02.110
to Anthos.

00:18:02.110 --> 00:18:04.770
The screen shows an
example of deploying

00:18:04.770 --> 00:18:06.790
one of the very popular
applications, which

00:18:06.790 --> 00:18:10.498
I used to demonstrate the
capabilities of Cloud Foundry.

00:18:10.498 --> 00:18:11.540
It's called spring-music.

00:18:11.540 --> 00:18:15.010
And in this case, we are
deploying that application

00:18:15.010 --> 00:18:16.150
using kf.

00:18:16.150 --> 00:18:17.770
And the developer
expedience will

00:18:17.770 --> 00:18:20.800
be pretty much
similar to what they

00:18:20.800 --> 00:18:24.075
will see with Cloud Foundry.

00:18:24.075 --> 00:18:25.700
Once you have deployed
the application,

00:18:25.700 --> 00:18:29.170
now you get to the portion
where you're operating it.

00:18:29.170 --> 00:18:33.530
So developers can use the
various command line arguments,

00:18:33.530 --> 00:18:35.690
which are required,
which are available,

00:18:35.690 --> 00:18:39.560
to operate the applications
which they move from PCF to kf.

00:18:39.560 --> 00:18:43.460
And operators can use
the kf installation

00:18:43.460 --> 00:18:48.560
with GKE and Anthos tools
to manage the deployments.

00:18:48.560 --> 00:18:51.390
So here, you see examples
of some of the kf

00:18:51.390 --> 00:18:54.830
commands developers can use
to look at their applications.

00:18:54.830 --> 00:18:58.130
They can use kf scale to
scale out their applications.

00:18:58.130 --> 00:19:01.190
They can use kf logs to look
at the logs that are generated

00:19:01.190 --> 00:19:03.420
by their application.

00:19:03.420 --> 00:19:08.400
And operators can use
the various UIs, which

00:19:08.400 --> 00:19:11.910
are available, to manage
GKE and Cloud Run,

00:19:11.910 --> 00:19:14.790
to actually see what the
status of an application is.

00:19:14.790 --> 00:19:16.770
It provides them a
single pane of glass

00:19:16.770 --> 00:19:20.020
for managing both their
configuration and policies,

00:19:20.020 --> 00:19:22.020
both on-prem and in the cloud.

00:19:22.020 --> 00:19:25.120
So they can look at their
configuration and policies,

00:19:25.120 --> 00:19:27.420
as well as get deep visibility
into their applications

00:19:27.420 --> 00:19:31.040
through services and network.

00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:35.060
And your call to action today
is please, review the resources,

00:19:35.060 --> 00:19:37.670
which are linked as
part of the session.

00:19:37.670 --> 00:19:40.250
If you are interested in
learning more about our PCF

00:19:40.250 --> 00:19:44.090
migration strategy, please
reach out to your account team

00:19:44.090 --> 00:19:46.460
and please, talk
to us about how we

00:19:46.460 --> 00:19:51.828
can kickstart a pilot for
migrating from PCF to Anthos.

00:19:51.828 --> 00:19:53.370
With that, I wrap
up my presentation.

00:19:53.370 --> 00:19:55.870
Thank you, for joining me today.

